From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: send the flip event after the vblank irq in video mode

In video mode the commit thread waits for the CTL flush register to
clear and then sends the page-flip event with the last vblank count and
timestamp. The register can clear before the vblank irq of the frame
that took the flush has run. On fixed refresh that is rare, 1 flip in
2700 on the Nova. With AVR it is common: a commit made during a held
front porch starts its frame within a tenth of a millisecond, and 37 of
2700 flips carried the previous frame's count and time, so two flips in
a row reported the same vblank.

Wait for pending_kickoff_cnt as well. The vblank irq counts it down
only once the hardware has taken this kickoff's flush bits, which stay
in the software mask until the next commit, and only after it has
updated the vblank count and timestamp.

Source: https://github.com/portare-ch/portareos/blob/be06100b6d127692ed99401d23980fc04888ae01/projects/PortareOS/devices/SM8550/patches/linux/1098-drm-msm-dpu-send-the-flip-event-after-the-vblank-irq.patch
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder_phys_vid.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder_phys_vid.c
@@ -607,8 +607,18 @@
 	if (!hw_ctl)
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * Wait for the vblank irq to count the kickoff down, not only for the
+	 * flush register to clear. The irq updates the vblank count and
+	 * timestamp first, so the flip event sent after this wait carries the
+	 * frame that took the flush. The register alone can clear before that
+	 * irq has run: with AVR a commit made during a held front porch starts
+	 * its frame at once, and 37 of 2700 flips went out with the previous
+	 * frame's count and time.
+	 */
 	ret = wait_event_timeout(phys_enc->pending_kickoff_wq,
-		(hw_ctl->ops.get_flush_register(hw_ctl) == 0),
+		(hw_ctl->ops.get_flush_register(hw_ctl) == 0 &&
+		 atomic_read(&phys_enc->pending_kickoff_cnt) == 0),
 		msecs_to_jiffies(50));
 	if (ret <= 0) {
 		DPU_ERROR("vblank timeout: %x\n", hw_ctl->ops.get_flush_register(hw_ctl));
